{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== TRIAGE: finding 1 (idempotence / converged-pair churn) — ROOT CAUSE PROVEN, AND IT IS A\nPRODUCT DEFECT. It is NOT in the outbound differ. ===\n\nTHE PLAN ENTRY IS NOT FROM `outbound_differ`. That was the wrong suspect, and the entry itself\nsays so. Run 30724309686 reported\n`{'direction':'outbound','action':'update','target':'RBJQFTF-1','local_id':'RBJQFTF-1'}` — note\n`local_id` IS THE JIRA KEY. An `outbound_differ` update carries\n`provenance[\"local_id\"] = om.local_id`, the LOCAL ticket id (`run_differs.py:562-566`), which\nhere would be `jira-rbjqftf-1`. Only ONE emitter in the codebase produces `local_id == target ==\n<jira key>`: `differ._derive_provenance` (`differ.py:130-131`), whose last-resort fallback is\n`local_id = target`. So the churn comes from the LEGACY SNAPSHOT DIFFER, not the field differ.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, end to end:\n\n1. `run_differs.py:222` calls `ctx.differ.compute_mutations(ctx.prev_snapshot, ctx.curr_snapshot)`\n   — it feeds the PREVIOUS PASS'S REMOTE SNAPSHOT into the parameter that differ.py's own module\n   docstring (`differ.py:5-11`) defines as \"the local source-of-truth state\", and which it says\n   REPLACED the legacy `compute_mutations(prev_snapshot, next_snapshot)` contract. Production is\n   still calling it with the legacy argument.\n2. `_compute_mutations_emit_both` (`differ.py:556-607`) then diffs every field not in\n   `EXCLUDED_FIELDS = (\"local_id\",\"rebar-id\")` (`config.py:5`) and, on any difference, emits\n   `(outbound, update)` with `changed[field] = local_val` — i.e. the STALE prev-snapshot value.\n   Read against the real arguments this means: any change to the Jira issue between two passes is\n   emitted as an outbound update that would push the OLD value back.\n3. `reconcile.py:613` advances the prev snapshot with `shutil.copy2(curr_path, prev_path)` — the\n   PRE-APPLY fetch. So an outbound write applied during pass N is invisible to `prev` at pass N+1.\n   The comment one line above (`reconcile.py:609`) states the intent it fails to meet: \"Advance\n   prev snapshot so the next call converges to zero mutations.\"\n4. Therefore, one pass after OUR OWN outbound summary write lands, prev(old) vs curr(new) differ\n   and the phantom is emitted. `persist` is False in a dry-run, so prev NEVER advances and the\n   dry-run re-plans it forever. That is exactly what the cell reports.\n\nREPRODUCED LOCALLY, NO JIRA NEEDED — byte-identical to the harness output:\n\n    prev = {\"RBJQFTF-1\": {...,\"summary\": OLD, ...}}      # persisted pre-apply by pass N\n    curr = {\"RBJQFTF-1\": {...,\"summary\": NEW, ...}}      # the fetch now sees our own write\n    differ.compute_mutations(prev, curr)\n    -> [{'direction':'outbound','action':'update','target':'RBJQFTF-1',\n         'local_id':'RBJQFTF-1','payload':{'summary': OLD}}]\n\n    control: compute_mutations(curr, curr) -> []\n\nThe payload is the OLD summary: the phantom proposes to REVERT the write the cell just made.\n\nIT IS INERT AT APPLY TIME, WHICH IS WHY PRODUCTION NEVER NOTICED. The payload is a bare field\ndict, not `{\"changed_fields\": ...}`, so `batch_dispatch._mutation_to_batch_dict:100-103` resolves\n`fields = payload.get(\"changed_fields\") or payload.get(\"fields\", {})` to `{}` and\n`dispatch_one._update_one_scalar_update:640-650` issues an empty `update_issue(key)`. Nothing\nchanges on the instance. So the mutation can never be \"done\" — it is planned every pass, counts\nagainst the BOOTSTRAP_STRICT cap of 10 (`mode.py:104-111`), inflates `mutation_count`, and makes\n\"converged\" a lie. Unconvergeable by construction. (Do NOT \"fix\" it by correcting the payload\nshape: that would make it apply, and it would then revert every remote edit and flip-flop.)\n\nONE CAUSE EXPLAINS ALL THIRTEEN OF RUN 3's IDEMPOTENCE FAILURES, in both directions — the trigger\nis \"the DC issue changed since the last persisted fetch\", regardless of which side changed it or\nwhich field. inbound 05/06-label, 08/09-assignee, 03-description and outbound 02/03/05/07/08 all\nfit without exception.\n\nTHIS IS A SIBLING OF AN ALREADY-CLOSED BUG, which settles the legitimacy question. `differ.py:80-92`\nplus the label branch at `differ.py:576-582` were added by ticket robe-creek-zealot and describe\nthis exact mechanism verbatim: \"The snapshot differ compares the PREVIOUS pass's Jira snapshot\nagainst the current one, so our own label write-back from the prior pass surfaces here as apparent\nremote drift one pass later — and emitted a spurious (outbound, update) echo\". That fix was applied\nto LABELS ONLY; every other field still churns. Same finding as the 9263 comment on this ticket:\non finding a defect class, sweep for siblings. Story 26de's contract is also explicit\n(`differ.py:688-691`): \"a write recorded as local-origin on pass N must not re-emit on pass N+1\neven if the jira snapshot has not yet caught up.\"\n\nTHE SAME EMITTER ALSO EXPLAINS FINDING 6 (deleted issue plans no inbound probe). Its failure text\nshows `{'direction':'outbound','action':'create','target':'RBJWUOX-1','local_id':'RBJWUOX-1'}` —\nthe same `local_id == target == key` signature. Deleting the issue drops it out of `curr`, and a\nkey present in prev-but-not-curr is read by this differ as \"local has it, Jira does not\" -> an\noutbound CREATE proposing to RE-CREATE the issue we just deleted. Finding 6 should be triaged\nagainst the same emitter rather than against the probe path.\n\nREMEDIATION, filed separately as a bug under e369. The narrow fix is to stop the snapshot differ's\nfield-drift outbound updates from reaching the mutation list, at the `run_differs.py:222` call\nsite, and NOT to touch `differ.compute_mutations`'s documented local-vs-jira contract (its unit\ntests legitimately exercise that contract with real local state). For a bound pair, field sync in\nboth directions is already owned by `_run_differs_outbound` / `_run_differs_inbound`, which run\nimmediately after; the snapshot differ's contribution there is pure noise. No J11 test change is\nneeded: the cell is correct and its diagnosis (\"this is real churn\") was right."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785644788981215001,"uuid":"ba3dd870-2067-45f6-8d3c-6cf7c13c60aa"}